velength  so if it's high in the uvb it absorbs  uvb more strongly  if it's higher in the uva it absorbs uva  more strongly  these are all comparing the same mass  concentration of the sunscreen they're  all one percent  in ethanol so this is very roughly  reflected  in the actives in an actual sunscreen  product but you do have to remember that  these curves don't completely reflect  what happens in a sunscreen product  these are for the sunscreen filters  dissolved in ethanol  when you actually put a sunscreen on  your skin the ethanol evaporates pretty  quickly  the sunscreen also dries down into a  film rather than floating around  in liquid which is what's happening in  these curves and so that also changes  the optical properties of the film  and remember there are also lots of  things like ph and solvent that could  change  those energy gaps and so that means that  the curve might shift to the left might  shift to the right  might shift up and down a bit it gets  even more complicated if you put more  than one filter into a single sunscreen  if you have a uvb filter and a uva  filter then having them both in there  means that you can get a broad spectrum  curve  so that means for the overall sunscreen  the final curve  will look very different from these  it'll be sort of like you add them  together  when i say add together i don't mean you  can just sum them up the final curve can  be a bit tricky to predict  the filters might interact  synergistically so the curve ends up  higher than you'd expect  they might change each other's  delocalization and so the curve might  shift left and right there's lots and  lots of possibilities that you can't  really predict  again these curves are for one percent  in a solution not  whatever percentage in your actual  sunscreen product if you have a higher  percentage the curve will go up because  you have more sunscreen molecules on  your skin  the sunscreen molecules can absorb more  uv and so you have  higher absorbance another thing to  notice is that it doesn't matter how  many filters you have to build that  final curve  what matters is just that final curve  i've seen some skin care science  influencers say that you need more  filters for redundancy but that's not  how uv absorbance works  it's like saying that a 2 meter ladder  made of steel aluminium and wood  will get you higher than a two meter  ladder just made of steel  they both get you two meters it doesn't  matter what materials they're made of  [Music]  now to the inorganic sunscreen zinc  oxide and titanium dioxide  back in the day the cosmetics industry  thought that they worked just by  scattering uv  it was actually written into the 1978  fda legislation and then taken out when  they revised it in 1999  so that's probably why this is so  widespread but scientists have known  that it works by mostly absorbing uv  for quite a long time and scientists  keep pointing it out in peer-reviewed  papers but it's just one of those myths  that  won't die the way that zinc oxide and  titanium dioxide work  is very much like an organic sunscreen  so they have  electrons that can absorb uv and get  excited into a higher state  then when those electrons relax back  down that uv energy converts to more  harmless forms  so heat or light or lower energy uv  or infrared but zinc oxide and titanium  dioxide are  inorganic that means they don't have  carbon atoms in their structure  so they don't have the same ability to  make conjugated systems  instead what they have is called a  bandgap structure  so here is what zinc oxide looks like  there are a lot of atoms packed closely  together which means lots of electrons  and so those electrons can interact with  each other so we get lots and lots of  different energy levels for lots of  different electrons  so instead of a low energy level and a  high energy level you have  a low energy band made up of lots of  levels  and a high energy band made up of lots  of levels the smallest gap between them  from the highest low energy level  to the lowest high energy level is  the minimum absorbance so the smallest  energy gap that can be absorbed  so that's the minimum energy or longest  wavelength they can absorb  and they can absorb pretty much every uv  energy level higher than this so every  shorter wavelength of uv  and that's because of that band  structure the fact there's so many  different levels there's lots of  different energy transitions that can  happen  so that's why the absorbance curves look  like they suddenly go off a cliff  that cliff matches the shortest energy  gap  now this absorbance explains about 95 of  how they work  the other approximately 5 is scattering  scattering is when the uv comes in and  the particle changes the direction that  it's going in  it doesn't actually usually bounce  backward it usually bounces forwards  so it keeps on going but at a different  angle so a lot of the time uv light  actually gets scattered towards your  skin  which is actually okay because it means  that it takes a longer path it's not  taking the shortest path anymore  and that means there's a higher chance  of that uv hitting another particle and  getting absorbed  now inorganic sunscreens are pretty  complicated because you can get  different particle sizes  in sunscreens the particle size is  usually really small less than 200  nanometers  that's because scattering is  proportional to diameter the bigger it  is the more it scatters the less it  absorbs  it's not only uv that gets scattered  it's also visible light and so the  larger the particle  the more scattering you get the more  visible light gets scattered into your  eye from the sunscreen  and you see it as a white cast there's  also the fact that only atoms at the  surface of the particle can absorb uv  if you have a big particle and you break  it down into smaller particles you get  more surface exposed and so you get  greater uv  absorbance longer wavelengths also get  scattered more than shorter wavelengths  so  even though the particles are mostly  absorbing the uv  they can also at the same time be  scattering visible light zinc oxide  absorbs both uva and uvb  titanium dioxide mostly absorbs uvb and  mostly scatters uva  but it does depend on the particular  particle size of the sunscreen  i'm going to leave it there if you got  to this point congratulations well done  i am very proud here is your prize the  cutest thing i've seen in a long time  these two penguins both had their  partners pass away but  every night they meet up to watch the  city lights and comfort each other for  hours  if you have any questions drop them down  below and i'll try my best to answer  them  if you like the video click the like and  subscribe you can also follow me on  instagram at lab muffin beauty science  and check out my blog for more nerdy  beauty content  see you next time to nerd out more
 

